---
/**
 * @component Hero
 * @version   3.2.0
 * @updated   2026-09-11
 * @source    astro-template@0ad4fd0
 * @copied    2026-09-10
 * @modified  true
 *
 * The opening screen: a cut-out figure standing in the name.
 *
 * The composition is a single idea — the name set as large as the measure
 * allows, and a cut-out figure standing beside and slightly over it, so the two
 * read as one image rather than as a picture next to a heading. Everything else
 * (the numbered roles, the handwritten opening line) is small, which is what
 * leaves the page to the name and the photograph.
 *
 * WHY THE FIGURE IS BESIDE THE NAME AND NOT CENTRED ON IT. The reference for
 * this hero centres the figure over the name, and it works there because the
 * name is ghosted to a pale tint the photograph can sit on top of. This name
 * is the page's `h1` and stays in ink, so nothing may cover it: the figure is
 * an opaque cut-out, and centred it would take "WILLI—" out of "WILLIAM" and
 * leave the heading to be guessed at. So the figure moves off to the side and
 * overlaps only the empty run at the end of the line.
 *
 * On a phone there is no room to put anything beside anything, so the two
 * stack and nothing overlaps: the name, the figure under it, then the roles
 * and the buttons. The overlap is what makes the pair read as one image when
 * they stand side by side, and it does not survive the translation — at 390
 * the picture is nearly the width of the screen, so type laid over it reads as
 * a caption printed on a photograph rather than as a composition. The name
 * leads and the face follows, which is the order the hero is read in anyway.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE CUT-OUT — read this before swapping the portrait.
 *
 * `portrait-bw.png` is a real cut-out: greyscale plus an alpha channel, and
 * nothing on this page removes a background. The matte was computed from the
 * studio shot on its white backdrop by `scripts/cutout-portrait.py`, which is
 * where the arithmetic lives, along with the flat-field correction a delivered
 * photo is likely to need first — this one arrived with a lighting gradient
 * behind the subject running 231 at the top of the frame to 251 at the bottom,
 * and every one of those levels reads as thin foreground.
 *
 * It used to be done here instead: the JPEG on its white backdrop, composited
 * with `mix-blend-mode: multiply`, which keeps the darker of the two layers so
 * white became page and the figure stayed. That rendered correctly and cost
 * one CSS property, and it is worth knowing what it demanded, because the
 * markup below was shaped around it:
 *
 *   - NO ANCESTOR OF THE IMAGE COULD FORM A STACKING CONTEXT. An element only
 *     blends with the backdrop inside the nearest isolating ancestor, so a
 *     `z-index`, an `opacity` below 1, a `transform`, a `filter` or a
 *     `will-change` anywhere up the tree left the figure blending against
 *     nothing and rendering as a plain white box.
 *   - The figure could not be animated, since `rise-in` animates `transform`.
 *   - The page under it had to stay light, or multiply ate the figure instead
 *     of the backdrop.
 *   - The backdrop in the file had to be white, not merely light. A backdrop
 *     at 231 is a visible grey box.
 *
 * None of that binds any more — the figure is an ordinary image that happens
 * to be person-shaped, and it is now free to be animated, wrapped or layered.
 * The layout below still does its stacking with DOM order alone, because that
 * is a perfectly good way to say what overlaps what, but it is no longer a
 * constraint and adding a `z-index` no longer breaks anything.
 *
 * Two things do still bind:
 *
 *   1. `showPlaceholder={false}` is not optional. CustomPicture's blur-up
 *      layer is a scaled, blurred copy of the photo painted BEHIND the image,
 *      and an image you can see through shows it — the figure would stand in a
 *      grey ghost of himself.
 *   2. The fallback format has to carry alpha. JPEG cannot, so CustomPicture
 *      serves PNG under the WebP for any source with an alpha channel; that is
 *      automatic, but it is the reason a `<picture>` here is not the usual
 *      WebP + JPEG pair.
 *
 * `grayscale` is applied in CSS as well and is a genuine no-op: the file
 * measures a mean chroma spread of 0.0, i.e. it is already true greyscale. It
 * stays as a guard, because the obvious way to break the look later is to drop
 * a colour photograph in and not notice.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE ENTRANCE.
 *
 * One cascade, in reading order: the opening line, the given name, the
 * surname, the three roles, then the buttons. The delays below are a sequence
 * and are meant to stay one — they drifted out of order once already, with the
 * buttons arriving in the middle of the roles list, which reads as a page
 * assembling itself rather than as a page being laid out.
 *
 * The opening line gets its own animation rather than `rise-in`: a mask sweeps
 * across it left to right, so the one line on the site set in handwriting
 * arrives as though it were being written. See `write-in` in the Tailwind
 * config for the geometry, which the mask utilities on that paragraph have to
 * agree with.
 *
 * The mask's resting position is the one that shows the whole line. That is
 * deliberate and load-bearing: `motion-safe:` removes the animation, not the
 * mask, so a resting position of "hidden" would leave a reader who asked for
 * less movement looking at a permanently half-masked sentence.
 *
 * The figure is deliberately not in the cascade. It used to be barred from it
 * — a `transform` on the blended image collapsed the cut-out — and that bar is
 * gone with the blend, but the composition still wants the photograph simply
 * to be there while the text assembles itself in front of it.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * ONE NUMBER TO KNOW: `FIGURE_WIDTH`.
 *
 * `src/assets/portrait-bw.png` is 400x400 and `CustomPicture` never upscales,
 * so 400px of real detail is all there is. The subject fills 97% x 96% of that
 * frame, so there is no margin to crop away and nothing to reclaim — the only
 * lever is how far the file gets stretched.
 *
 * It is set below to a size that stretches it about 1.25x, which a soft-edged
 * black-and-white figure carries but a crisp framed colour photo would not.
 * That is why this hero is as large as it is and no larger.
 *
 * Note the phone and tablet sizes are DOWNscales of the same file, so they are
 * pin sharp; only the `lg` size stretches it.
 *
 * A ~1400px portrait on the same white backdrop, run through
 * `scripts/cutout-portrait.py`, lets this go to the full reference
 * proportions: raise the `lg` step of `FIGURE_WIDTH` and update `sizes` to
 * match. Nothing else in this file needs to change.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import type { ImageMetadata } from "astro";
import CustomPicture from "@ui/CustomPicture.astro";
import TextRender from "@ui/TextRender.astro";
import Button from "@ui/Button.astro";
import AmbientWash from "@ui/AmbientWash.astro";

export interface ImageItem {
  image: ImageMetadata | string;
  description?: string;
  subText?: string;
}

export interface HeroLink {
  label: string;
  link: string;
}

export interface Props {
  /** The name. This is the headline — not a job title. */
  title?: string;
  /** One sentence that sounds like a person wrote it. */
  intro?: string;
  /** The numbered list beside the figure. Three. */
  roles?: string[];
  /** First is rendered solid, the rest outlined. Two is plenty. */
  ctas?: HeroLink[];
  imageItems?: ImageItem[];
}

const {
  title = "",
  intro = "",
  roles = [],
  ctas = [],
  imageItems = [],
} = Astro.props;

const portrait = imageItems[0];

/** See the long note above before changing this. */
const FIGURE_WIDTH = "w-full sm:w-[22rem] lg:w-[33rem]";
/**
 * Pulls the figure back over the text column so the two overlap. A margin
 * rather than a transform, so the figure's box moves with it and the name's
 * column reflows around it — a transform would slide the picture over a column
 * that still believes it is the full width.
 */
const FIGURE_OVERLAP = "lg:-ml-16";

/**
 * The name, split into the given name and everything after it.
 *
 * Set on two lines rather than left to wrap: at this size the column would
 * break "William van der Vegt" wherever it happened to run out, and a name is
 * not a paragraph. The split is on the first space, so any given name plus any
 * remainder works and a single-word name degrades to one line.
 *
 * The two halves are then set as a deliberate pair rather than as one long
 * headline — see the markup. The given name is the loud one; the surname is
 * quieter, lighter and in its own case, which is also why the tussenvoegsels
 * are left alone: "van der Vegt" is how the name is actually written, and
 * shouting it in capitals throws away that detail.
 */
const space = title.indexOf(" ");
const given = space > -1 ? title.slice(0, space) : title;
const family = space > -1 ? title.slice(space + 1) : "";
---

<section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-hairline bg-background">
  {/* The washes. A SIBLING painted before the content, never an ancestor of
      it: they are atmosphere, and everything on this page has to sit in front
      of them. See AmbientWash's header note.

      The graph paper that used to sit in this corner is gone: the washes do
      the atmosphere here now, and the ruled pad reads as far more purposeful
      where it was kept, behind the homelab parts table. */}
  <AmbientWash intensity="strong" />

  <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 lg:px-8">
    {/* --- The opening line ------------------------------------------------
        In the handwriting face: the one sentence on the page that should read
        as written rather than set.

        It runs larger than the body copy because it has to — Caveat's x-height
        is small, and at body size a script face stops being informal and
        starts being hard to read. `leading-snug` pulls the lines back in,
        since the larger size would otherwise open the block up more than a
        note should be.

        It sits alone up here now. There used to be a mono strip of keywords
        opposite it (software / cloud / homelab / maken / fotografie), which
        said in five nouns what this sentence already says in one sentence, in
        a voice the sentence is there to avoid. `sm:ml-auto` is what holds the
        line to the right-hand column without a flex row to justify it, so it
        still lands above the portrait. */}
    {
      intro && (
        <p
          class="max-w-sm text-pretty pt-10 font-hand text-[1.4rem] leading-snug text-blackText [mask-image:linear-gradient(90deg,black_33.3%,transparent_43.3%)] [mask-position:0%_0] [mask-repeat:no-repeat] [mask-size:300%_100%] motion-safe:animate-write-in sm:ml-auto sm:text-right sm:text-[1.55rem]"
          style="animation-delay: 60ms;"
        >
          <TextRender>{intro}</TextRender>
        </p>
      )
    }

    {/* --- Name and figure ------------------------------------------------
        Two columns, bottom-aligned, so the figure lands on the section's
        bottom edge and bleeds off it while the text column stops short. The
        figure carries a negative left margin so it encroaches on the name
        rather than sitting politely next to it.

        Note the complete absence of `z-index`: paint order is DOM order (text
        column first, figure second, so the figure wins). That is deliberate
        and it is enough; it was once also mandatory, which the header note
        explains. */}
    <div class="mt-6 flex flex-col pb-12 lg:mt-4 lg:flex-row lg:items-end lg:gap-4 lg:pb-0">
      {/* THE TEXT COLUMN IS `contents` BELOW `lg`, WHICH IS THE WHOLE TRICK.
          The name has to come before the figure on a phone and the roles after
          it — name, face, then the list — and `order` can only shuffle items
          that are siblings in the same flex box. `display: contents` drops
          this wrapper out of the box and promotes its children into it, so the
          h1, the roles and the buttons each get an `order` of their own and
          the figure can slot in between them. At `lg` it becomes a block
          again, the three are ordinary children of it, `order` stops applying,
          and the two-column row is exactly as it was.

          The figure and the name do NOT overlap on a phone. They did, with the
          name pulled up into the shirt; at 390 the picture is nearly as wide
          as the screen, so that read as type dropped on a photograph rather
          than as one composition — which is what the overlap does well at `lg`
          and cannot do here. The overlap is now a desktop idea only, and it is
          the `lg:-ml-16` that states it. */}
      <div class="contents lg:block lg:min-w-0 lg:flex-1 lg:pb-12">
        {/* Deliberately wider than the column it sits in, so the longest line
            runs on under the figure rather than stopping short of it. The
            section's `overflow-hidden` is what keeps that from widening the
            page. */}
        <h1 class="order-1 lg:w-[42rem]">
          {/* The given name: the loud half. Capitals, heavy, tracked in tight
              enough that the letters nearly touch. */}
          <span
            class="block text-[clamp(3rem,14vw,7.5rem)] font-semibold uppercase leading-[0.82] tracking-[-0.04em] text-primaryText motion-safe:animate-rise-in"
            style="animation-delay: 170ms;"
          >
            <TextRender>{given}</TextRender>
          </span>

          {/* The surname: the quiet half, and deliberately a different voice
              rather than the same one turned down. Three things change at once
              — weight (semibold to light), case (capitals to the name as it is
              actually written), and colour (ink to muted) — because changing
              only one reads as an accident, and changing all three reads as a
              pair.

              It keeps roughly half the given name's size, which is small enough
              to be clearly subordinate and large enough to still hold the
              bottom of the block. The tracking goes the other way too: the
              given name is tightened to -0.04em, this is opened slightly, so
              the two halves sit differently on the line rather than looking
              like one headline in two weights. */}
          {
            family && (
              <span
                class="mt-2 block text-[clamp(1.75rem,7.2vw,3.9rem)] font-light leading-[1] tracking-[0.01em] text-mutedText motion-safe:animate-rise-in sm:mt-3"
                style="animation-delay: 280ms;"
              >
                <TextRender>{family}</TextRender>
              </span>
            )
          }
        </h1>

        {
          roles.length > 0 && (
            <ol class="order-3 mt-9 flex flex-col gap-1.5">
              {roles.map((role, i) => (
                <li
                  class="flex items-baseline gap-3 motion-safe:animate-rise-in"
                  style={`animation-delay: ${400 + i * 85}ms`}
                >
                  {/* The index is furniture, so it is hidden from assistive
                      tech — an ordered list already announces position, and
                      "zero one" read before each role is noise. */}
                  <span
                    class="font-mono text-xs tabular-nums text-mutedText"
                    aria-hidden="true"
                  >
                    ({String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")})
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-xl font-medium italic tracking-tight text-primaryText sm:text-2xl">
                    <TextRender>{role}</TextRender>
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ol>
          )
        }

        {/* Stacked and full width on a phone, inline from `sm` up. Left to
            wrap, the two buttons sat at their own content widths in a ragged
            column; equal blocks read as a deliberate pair and give a thumb
            something to aim at. */}
        {
          ctas.length > 0 && (
            <div
              class="order-4 mt-9 flex flex-col gap-3 motion-safe:animate-rise-in sm:flex-row sm:flex-wrap"
              style="animation-delay: 700ms;"
            >
              {ctas.map((cta, i) => (
                <Button
                  href={cta.link}
                  variant={i === 0 ? "primary" : "secondary"}
                  arrow={i === 0}
                  class="justify-center sm:justify-start"
                >
                  <TextRender>{cta.label}</TextRender>
                </Button>
              ))}
            </div>
          )
        }
      </div>

      {/* `order-2` below `lg` puts the figure between the name and the roles.
          Left in source order it lands after the buttons, at the bottom of the
          section — the headshot arriving last, as an afterthought, when it is
          supposed to be the thing you land on. Directly under the name it is
          still the second thing on the page and the name is still the first,
          which is the order the hero is read in anyway. */}
      {
        portrait?.image && (
          <div class:list={["order-2 mt-8 shrink-0 lg:order-none lg:mt-0", FIGURE_WIDTH, FIGURE_OVERLAP]}>
            {/* Quality is high on purpose: the file is a few hundred pixels
                square, so this costs single-digit KB, and it is the only
                photograph of a face above the fold. `showPlaceholder` must
                stay false — see the header note. */}
            {/* The bottom fade is mobile-only, and it earns its place.
                The source frame cuts the shoulders off square. On desktop that
                never shows, because the figure bleeds past the section's bottom
                edge — but with the figure moved up the page on a phone, the cut
                lands mid-scroll as a hard horizontal slice across his chest.
                Dissolving the last quarter into the page hides an arbitrary
                crop and suits a cut-out better than a straight edge does.

                It multiplies into the alpha the file already carries, so the
                fade is a fade of the figure, not of a rectangle drawn over
                him. Verified at 390 / 768 / 1440. */}
            <CustomPicture
              alt={portrait.description}
              classes="aspect-square w-full grayscale [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_bottom,black_74%,transparent_100%)] lg:[mask-image:none]"
              sizes="(max-width: 639px) 100vw, (max-width: 1023px) 352px, 528px"
              srcImagePath={portrait.image}
              priority={true}
              qualityOverride={86}
              showPlaceholder={false}
            />
          </div>
        )
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
